**Current Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system operating in a multi-user environment supporting role hierarchies, temporary role delegation, revocation of delegated roles, and concurrent request processing. Each user has an assigned security clearance (High, Medium, Low), which governs what classified requests they may submit or approve. Requests affect system objects classified similarly and must respect clearance constraints. Users with higher clearance can delegate roles temporarily to lower-clearance users if clearance constraints are met. Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or can be revoked by administrators. The system ensures authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, liveness of request processing, and safety under concurrency.

The new Emergency Module introduces an additional system mode (Emergency mode), triggered by administrators under specific conditions, altering request processing and role privileges temporarily. Emergency mode duration is modeled abstractly as a maximum allowed temporal bound with possible earlier termination.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
==========================================
- Users have a fixed security clearance level: High, Medium, or Low.
- Administrators have High clearance, but not all High-clearance users are administrators.
- Users may hold assigned or delegated roles; delegation respects minimum clearance requirements.
- Requests submitted by users affect one or more objects, each with classification level High, Medium, or Low.
- The classification of a request is the highest classification among affected objects.
- Users may submit requests only if request classification ≤ user clearance.
- Higher-clearance users may approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Requests are processed concurrently in FIFO order.
- Every submitted request is eventually processed.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or can be revoked by administrators.
- Role delegation is temporary.
- The system preserves authorization correctness, permission consistency, and concurrency safety.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
=======================
R1: Emergency Mode Triggering  
While in Normal mode, exactly two administrators acting concurrently are required to trigger the system to enter Emergency mode.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
Emergency mode is active only for a limited (finite) time, modeled abstractly as a maximum allowed temporal bound, with possible earlier termination, after which the system must return to Normal mode.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
During Emergency mode:  
  R3.1: Request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize simultaneous processing of multiple requests in order of classification priority: high-classification requests first, then medium-classification requests, then low-classification requests.  
  R3.2: Users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles concurrently; specifically, mutually exclusive role pairs are allowed to be held simultaneously during Emergency mode.  
  R3.3: Administrators temporarily hold emergency privileges, abstracted as a special privilege set distinct from existing privileges.

R4: Post-Emergency Mode Actions  
After Emergency mode ends:  
  R4.1: Emergency privileges must be revoked from administrators.  
  R4.2: Roles delegated during the emergency must be revoked; delegated roles assigned prior to Emergency mode remain held during Emergency mode unless expired or explicitly revoked by an administrator.  
  R4.3: Administrators must update their credentials immediately after returning to Normal mode from Emergency mode.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An individual in the system with an assigned security clearance and roles.  
- Administrator: A user with High clearance and administrative privileges.  
- Security Clearance: Levels {High, Medium, Low} assigned to users.  
- Role: A set of permissions assigned or delegated to users; roles may be mutually exclusive.  
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily assigned to a user by a higher-clearance user, subject to clearance constraints and expiration or revocation.  
- Request: An action submitted by a user affecting one or more system objects.  
- Object: An entity affected by requests, each with a classification level {High, Medium, Low}.  
- Request Classification: The highest classification level among all objects affected by a request.  
- System Mode: The system operates in Normal or Emergency mode.  
- Emergency Privileges: Special privileges assigned temporarily to administrators during Emergency mode, abstracted as a special privilege set.  
- FIFO Order: First-In, First-Out processing order for requests in Normal mode.  
- Mutual Exclusion of Roles: Roles that cannot be held simultaneously by the same user in Normal mode but may be temporarily held simultaneously during Emergency mode for exclusive role pairs.  
- Emergency Duration: The finite time period the system remains in Emergency mode, modeled as a maximum temporal bound with possible earlier termination.  

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. What is the precise definition of "limited time" for Emergency mode duration? Is it a fixed known duration or a bounded interval?  
   - Clarified: Modeled abstractly as a maximum allowed temporal bound with possible earlier termination.

2. Are the "emergency privileges" for administrators distinct from existing privileges, and is their scope or nature defined?  
   - Clarified: Abstracted as a special privilege set distinct from existing privileges.

3. Regarding mutual exclusive roles during Emergency mode (R3.2), is mutual exclusivity suspended entirely or only partially? Are there specified role pairs or classes?  
   - Clarified: Exclusive pairs of roles are allowed to be held concurrently during Emergency mode.

4. For role delegation revocation after Emergency mode (R4.2), should all delegated roles (including those delegated before Emergency mode) be revoked, or only those delegated during Emergency mode?  
   - Clarified: Only roles delegated during Emergency mode are revoked; roles delegated before Emergency mode remain held unless expired or explicitly revoked by administrators.

5. For request processing in Emergency mode (R3.1), is prioritization restricted only to high-classification requests, or are other classification levels affected?  
   - Clarified: High-classification requests are processed first, then medium-classification requests, then low-classification requests.

6. Is the requirement that exactly 2 administrators trigger Emergency (R1) intended as a concurrent action, or can these two triggers be sequential within a short time window?  
   - Clarified: It is a concurrent action.

7. What is the mechanism or process for administrators updating credentials after Emergency mode (R4.3)? Is this enforced immediately after Emergency mode or within a grace period?  
   - Clarified: It is an event that must happen immediately after returning to Normal mode from Emergency mode.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- During Normal mode, requests are processed in FIFO order; in Emergency mode, this order can be bypassed for prioritizing requests by classification in order: high, then medium, then low.  
- Users may only submit requests with classification ≤ their clearance.  
- Delegated roles must meet minimum clearance requirements of the role and expire or can be revoked.  
- Emergency mode activation requires exactly two administrators' concurrent triggers while in Normal mode.  
- Emergency mode duration is finite, modeled as a maximum temporal bound with possible earlier termination; system must eventually return to Normal mode.  
- During Emergency mode, mutual exclusivity constraints on exclusive role pairs are relaxed temporarily, allowing concurrent holding.  
- Emergency privileges are assigned to administrators only during Emergency mode and revoked afterward.  
- After Emergency mode, all roles delegated during the emergency must be revoked; roles delegated before Emergency mode remain unless expired or revoked.  
- After Emergency mode, administrators must update credentials immediately before resuming normal operations.  
- Concurrency and liveness properties of request processing must be preserved across modes.  
